**
Cobalt Strike beacons employ **Malleable C2 profiles** to mimic legitimate protocols, thwarting signature-based detection. Key evasion techniques include:

1. **Jitter & Chunked Encoding:** Cobalt Strike fragments HTTP responses into random-sized chunks (e.g., 512, 2048 bytes) using `http-chunked` parameter.
   ```json
   http-chunked;
   set uri "/wp-content/plugins/hello.php?p=%s";
   client {
     header "Accept" "text/html";
     metadata {
       netbios;
       uri-full;
     }
   }
   ```

2. **Domain Fronting via HTTP Host Header:** Beacons route traffic through benign domains (e.g., `cdn.ms`) by setting:
   ```json
   host "ajax.googleapis.com";
   header "Host" "real-target.com";
   ```

3. **TLS Fingerprint Spoofing:** Malleable C2 profiles mimic browser TLS ClientHello via `tls-fingerprint`:
   ```json
   tls-fingerprint "firefox";
   ```

**Snort 3 Rule Breakdown:**
Original Snort rule:
```ruby
alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg:"HTTP Cobalt Strike"; content:"POST /index.php"; http_header; sid:1000001;)
```
Evasion succeeds because Cobalt Strike uses `/wp-content/plugins/hello.php`. A bypassed rule must include **HTTP URI normalization**:
```ruby
alert tcp any any -> any 80 (
  msg:"Evasive C2 Detection";
  content:!"GET /";
  content:"/wp-content/plugins/hello.php";
  http_uri; http_method;
  pcre:"/^(POST|GET)\s+\/wp-content\/plugins\/hello\.php\?[a-zA-Z0-9%]{8,}$/";
  sid:1000002;
)
```
However, **JARM fingerprinting** (JA3S/JARM) detects inconsistent TLS fingerprints, forcing Cobalt Strike to adapt via custom TLS stacks (e.g., `minhook`-injected beacons).

